      "S4",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Gina van Asbeck is a Dutch property executive, also known as Gesina van Hoeven. The widow of developer Carel van Asbeck, she took over management roles in his business network. She represented the owners in plans to redevelop the Honig factory in Koog aan de Zaan and managed French property companies.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Gina van Asbeck's interest in gardening prompted a plan to give an unused paddock in Laren a new purpose. Inspired by the documentary The Biggest Little Farm, she wanted to develop a permaculture garden, combining plants that help sustain one another. She imagined paths through a flower meadow, with fruit trees and vegetables.\n\nIn 2022, she invited neighbours, gardening specialists and volunteers to help turn the idea into a practical project. She considered organising the work as a cooperative and beginning with part of the field. Her proposal sought both practical knowledge of soil and planting and people willing to help with construction.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Full first-person interview supports the proposed project; no completion or current operating status is claimed."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 103,
    "html": "<p>Gina van Asbeck's interest in gardening prompted a plan to give an unused paddock in Laren a new purpose. Inspired by the documentary The Biggest Little Farm, she wanted to develop a permaculture garden, combining plants that help sustain one another. She imagined paths through a flower meadow, with fruit trees and vegetables.</p>\r\n<p>In 2022, she invited neighbours, gardening specialists and volunteers to help turn the idea into a practical project. She considered organising the work as a cooperative and beginning with part of the field. Her proposal sought both practical knowledge of soil and planting and people willing to help with construction.</p>\r\n"
  },
